WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the summit's virtual detention reel u.s  president joe biden wanted to meet his  chinese counterpart xi jinping in person  but the chinese leader rejecting all  external invites  the fact the us wants to push ahead  flags the importance of a bilateral  relationship which has hit stalemate i  would argue that the u.s china  relationship is at one of its most  precarious moments that it's been in for  quite some time  china says it's ready to work with the  us to get the relationship back on track  the two super powers have shared  interests there's the fight against  covet the problems with the global  supply chain and global warming  joe biden has had two conversations with  xi jinping since he moved into the oval  office the first didn't happen until  february that delay seen by some as a  deliberate snub for beijing the second  conversation was in september but joe  biden was very critical of ji's decision  not to go to the climate summit in  glasgow  essentially saying that if you want to  be considered a world leader you have to  actually show up to lead  there are tensions between the two most  notably over the chinese crackdown on  pro-democracy protesters in hong kong  the treatment of mainly muslim uighurs  in china and chinese military exercises  near taiwan which the u.s has promised  to defend  one top u.s official says china's  economic growth is changing the global  landscape we're witnessing one of the  largest  shifts in global geostrategic power that  the world has witnessed and it only  happens once in a while china doesn't  want a conflict with the united states  just like the us doesn't want to come  with china and so i think that the idea  of a summit is  essentially an off-ramp it's an  opportunity for the two sides to  lower the temperatures and take stock of  where things are in the relationship  summits rarely happen without something  to show for it so there could be  movement on trade visa restrictions and  perhaps even small moves on nuclear  weapons both sides want to move from  confrontation to cooperation but both  sides need to agree that is the next  step alan fisher al jazeera at the white  house

